key: inferix
name: Inferix
url: https://inferix.co
tagline: The GPU cloud for AI

# One paragraph the copywriter is grounded on. Everything it claims must be
# traceable to this or to proof_points below.
about: >
  Inferix is a GPU cloud built for teams who want their models, their data and
  their compute in one place. Publish and version models and datasets with
  Git-style workflows. Launch Spaces to demo your work. Deploy
  OpenAI-compatible inference endpoints that drop into any existing SDK by
  changing a base URL. Rent verified GPU capacity by the hour, or list hardware
  you already own and earn from it. No proprietary model formats, no lock-in on
  your artifacts.

audience: >
  ML engineers, applied-AI developers and small AI teams who currently juggle
  Hugging Face for artifacts and a separate GPU marketplace for compute, plus
  GPU owners with idle consumer hardware looking to earn from it.

# Concrete, verifiable differentiators. The copywriter may only make claims
# that appear here - this is the guard against the model inventing benchmarks.
proof_points:
  - "OpenAI-compatible endpoints: change the base URL and existing SDK code works unmodified"
  - Models, datasets, Spaces and GPU rental in one account instead of two vendors
  - Per-second billing with no minimum commitment
  - Real verified GPU hardware rather than resold capacity
  - Inference router gives one endpoint for every routable model, with automatic fallback
  - Git-style versioning for models and datasets; artifacts stay portable
  - List hardware you already own and earn from it
  - Self-hosted agentic coding workspace (Inferix Coder) runs against your own model server

# Content pillars: the recurring subjects a feed is built from. Weighted -
# higher weight is chosen more often by the planner.
pillars:
  - key: ai_news
    weight: 3
    label: What is new in open AI
    description: >
      A specific, recent development in open models, inference tooling or GPU
      hardware, explained for a practitioner, with a concrete takeaway. Ties
      back to Inferix only where genuinely relevant.
  - key: tips
    weight: 3
    label: Tips and tricks working with AI
    description: >
      A single actionable technique - quantization tradeoffs, batching, KV
      cache, LoRA, prompt caching, serving throughput, VRAM math. Must contain
      something a reader can act on today.
  - key: cost
    weight: 2
    label: The economics of running models
    description: >
      Concrete cost and performance math for inference and training. Real
      numbers, honest tradeoffs, no manufactured comparisons against named
      competitors.
  - key: product
    weight: 2
    label: Product capability
    description: >
      One capability, shown rather than described - the endpoint swap, dataset
      versioning, Spaces, renting a verified GPU.
  - key: build_in_public
    weight: 1
    label: Build in public
    description: >
      Something learned building the platform, including things that went
      wrong. Honest engineering notes, not milestone celebration.

keywords:
  - open source LLM
  - GPU cloud
  - inference endpoint
  - vLLM
  - LoRA fine-tuning
  - model hosting
  - RTX 5090
  - self-hosted AI

cta_variants:
  - Free account at inferix.co
  - Try it at inferix.co
  - "Docs and free tier: inferix.co"
  - "Swap your base URL and see: inferix.co"

# Visual direction handed to the image model for every generated asset.
visual:
  style: >
    Clean technical product aesthetic. Deep near-black backgrounds, cyan and
    electric-blue accent light, subtle volumetric glow, crisp geometry.
    Datacenter and GPU hardware motifs, abstract compute topology, isometric
    server geometry. Sharp, high contrast, uncluttered, professional.
  negative: >
    stock photo people, handshake, generic businessman, cheesy, cluttered,
    watermark, text artifacts, garbled letters, low quality, blurry, oversaturated
  palette: ["#06b6d4", "#0ea5e9", "#0b1220", "#e2e8f0"]

voice:
  do:
    - Lead with a specific technical fact or number
    - Sound like an engineer who ships, talking to other engineers
    - Use plain concrete nouns; name the actual technology
    - Admit tradeoffs - it builds more trust than a clean pitch
  dont:
    - Hype adjectives - revolutionary, game-changing, cutting-edge, seamless, unleash
    - Rhetorical questions as openers
    - Emoji bullet lists as structure
    - Any claim not traceable to proof_points
